    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "baada198_9ee8b9b4",
        "filename": "keystone/common/ldap/core.py",
        "patchSetId": 15
      },
      "lineNbr": 702,
      "author": {
        "id": 708
      },
      "writtenOn": "2014-07-19T09:04:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Continuing discussion from previous patchset:\n\nPlease take a look here: http://git.openstack.org/cgit/openstack/keystone/tree/keystone/identity/backends/ldap.py#n66\n\nWhen user authenticates in Keystone, ones password is verified by doing bind on  a LDAP connection.",
      "revId": "827fcf33a6213b6076fba4032c20e939379998d7",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "baada198_81d059f8",
        "filename": "keystone/common/ldap/core.py",
        "patchSetId": 15
      },
      "lineNbr": 702,
      "author": {
        "id": 1091
      },
      "writtenOn": "2014-07-19T20:13:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "That is correct. If I recall, the concern you raised earlier was around password change. Ldap pool does match of existing connection creds and if not found, creates new bind for user cred data.\n\nNow there is no way to know whether user password is changed or is invalid. So in this case, if there is user password is modified, then no existing connection match found, so new bind is established.\n\nThere are unit test around authenticate method which executes in both pool versions..fake ldap and live ldap tests",
      "parentUuid": "baada198_9ee8b9b4",
      "revId": "827fcf33a6213b6076fba4032c20e939379998d7",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
